AI Clinical Trial Card: The Effects of Generative AI on High-Skilled Work: Evidence from Three Field Experiments with Software Developers

Authors: Ruoxi Cui et al. (Princeton / MIT / Microsoft)

AI Model Details

Provider: GitHub / OpenAI

Model Name and Version:

GitHub Copilot, powered by OpenAI’s Codex family of models

Exact underlying model version is not explicitly specified

Access Method:

GitHub Copilot was integrated directly into developers’ existing programming environments

Developers interacted with the AI through inline code suggestions as they wrote software

Fine-tuning:

Yes. Codex is fine-tuned on large-scale code repositories

No additional fine-tuning specific to the experimental tasks was reported

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Default Copilot configuration

Developers did not control temperature or decoding parameters

Performance on Standard Benchmarks:

Not reported

The study treats Copilot as a deployed commercial tool rather than evaluating the base model

Human Participant Profile

Sample Size:

Multiple field experiments; exact sample sizes vary by experiment and are reported separately in the paper

Population / Expertise:

Professional software developers

Participants are highly skilled workers, performing complex, real-world programming tasks

Domain Expertise:

Software engineering and code development

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Likely low-to-moderate

Experiments conducted during early diffusion of Copilot into professional developer workflows

Training / Onboarding:

Minimal onboarding

Developers received access to Copilot but no extensive training or prompting instruction

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Three field experiments examining developer performance on realistic programming tasks

Tasks were embedded in ongoing development work rather than isolated lab-style exercises

Interface:

Standard code editors with Copilot enabled

AI suggestions appeared inline and could be accepted, edited, or ignored

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive

Copilot suggested code snippets but did not autonomously commit or deploy code

Experimental Conditions:

Control: Copilot disabled

Treatment: Copilot enabled

Primary Outcome Measures:

Productivity, such as speed or volume of code-related work

Code quality, measured using task-specific or review-based metrics

Main Effects:

On average, access to Copilot increased developer productivity

Effects on code quality were heterogeneous:

Some tasks and developers experienced neutral or positive quality effects

Others exhibited quality trade-offs alongside productivity gains

Notes on Interpretation

This paper extends earlier Copilot studies by focusing on high-skilled professional developers rather than short, synthetic tasks

Results suggest that generative AI can improve productivity even for expert workers, but quality effects are not uniformly positive

The findings underscore the importance of evaluating multiple outcome dimensions (speed and quality) when assessing AI assistance in skilled work

Compared to lab experiments (e.g., Peng et al.), the field setting increases ecological validity but also introduces more heterogeneity in outcomes